{"kind":"artifact","text":"The browser decides for itself whether a remote may become an href, and links the repository path only when it may. Exact clean pushed head 95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb on request/repo-remote-link; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 59b9a2c0, checkout clean. Under planner's dd883da0 and my promise 29b95948. First artifact at this path from this lane.\n\nWHAT THIS PATH CHANGES. A new module, ui/src/lib/repolink.ts, holding repoRemoteHref — the one place a remote string is allowed to become an attribute the browser will navigate. api.ts types the field, store.ts carries it onto Workroom from the same one-shot poll, and TopBar.tsx renders an anchor when the function returns a URL and the previously existing span when it does not.\n\nWHY THE CHECK IS REPEATED HERE RATHER THAN TRUSTED. internal/app already refuses everything it will not link, and this layer refuses again. That is not redundancy: this is the site where a string becomes an href, and an older resident, a stale embed, or any future caller gets the same answer at the point it matters. The function returns the parser's own serialization rather than the caller's input, so what reaches the attribute is what the parser actually understood — a parser-differential between the Go and browser layers cannot smuggle anything through.\n\nTHE RULE. Parse, then admit only the http and https protocols. Everything else falls through to undefined and renders as plain text. Nothing is compared against a list of dangerous schemes, so being unrecognised is itself a refusal rather than a way past the check. Userinfo declines the link outright rather than being stripped, because a remote of the form https://x-access-token:SECRET@host/org/repo carries a credential: refusing keeps it out of the DOM entirely, where stripping would leave every later rendering path — href, title, any future copy affordance — responsible for dropping it again. The anchor opens a new tab and carries rel=\"noopener noreferrer\".\n\nSOMETHING THE CONDITIONS DID NOT ANTICIPATE, AND IT MAKES THIS CHECK LOAD-BEARING. React 19 does not make this redundant. Under mutation it replaced a javascript: URL with a throwing stub but still emitted an href attribute containing that URL, and a data: URL passed through verbatim into the attribute untouched. So the framework blocks one of the two schemes I care about, partially, and not the other. Anyone later reading this module as duplicated protection and deleting it would reopen the data: case silently.\n\nMUTATION, RUN BY ME. Replacing the allowlist with a permissive denylist fails four assertions, among them \"a script-bearing remote never reaches an href\" with the message that a javascript: URL carrying document.cookie reached an href attribute. Restored; git status empty and the tree hash unchanged at 59b9a2c0.\n\nTESTS, written from the desired behaviour rather than transcribed. An https remote links; git's non-web transports fall back to plain text; a script-bearing remote never becomes a link; a remote carrying userinfo does not leak into the DOM; and an unlinkable remote renders exactly as no remote does — that last one is the assertion that keeps the fallback honest rather than merely unlinked.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, from runs I watched exit. npm test --prefix ui 95 tests, 95 pass, 0 fail; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok plus 1 no-test-files, 35 packages, zero failures and zero cached; gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0 with no output; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb","path":"ui"}}